    {
      "query": "Bob challenge Bobcoin task session report judging",
      "results": [
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/account/bobcoins_public.md_chunk_0",
          "score": 0.8378973,
          "content": "Bobcoins are the consumption-based billing metric used for Bob plans. They provide a transparent, predictable way to measure and bill for your Bob usage.\n\nBobcoins are resource units that measure your consumption of Bob's AI capabilities. Each action you complete with Bob, such as generating code, analyzing files, or running commands, consumes a number of Bobcoins based on the computational resources required.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Bobcoins"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.0773781
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/features/bobalytics_public.md_chunk_10",
          "score": 0.8295431,
          "content": "Bobcoin spend tracks the total Bobcoins consumed over the selected period.\n\nPrimary metrics:\n\n- Impact and cost: How each team's Bob factor compares to their Bobcoin spend.\n\n![Impact and cost chart comparing Bob factor to Bobcoin spend](/images/spend-impact.png)\n\n- Daily spend: Bobcoins spent per day.\n\n![Daily Bobcoin spend chart over the selected time period](/images/spend-daily.png)\n\n- Team details and trends: Adoption, value, and cost metrics per team.\n\n![Team details and trends table with adoption, value, and cost metrics](/images/spend-trend.png)\n\nSupporting metrics:\n\n- Bob's repository impact: Repositories where Bob users have been active.\n\n**Tip:**\nIf a team is consuming Bobcoins faster than expected, review their adoption rate and Bob factor alongside spend. High spend paired with high adoption and a strong Bob factor suggests effective use. High spend with low Bob factor may indicate an opportunity to review how Bob is being used within that team.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Bobalytics \u003e Key performance indicators \u003e Bobcoin spend"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.022586042
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/core-concepts/context-window-management_public.md_chunk_7",
          "score": 0.84137154,
          "content": "Bobcoins track token usage. Both input and output tokens count.\n\n- Each message sends the full active context again, including fixed overhead.\n- Bob re-processes what is already loaded on every send.\n- Long threads with heavy **Messages** cost more per later prompt.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Context window management \u003e Impact on Bobcoins"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.016914913
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/enterprise/enterprise-index_public.md_chunk_0",
          "score": 6.3702393,
          "content": "Scale IBM Bob across your organization with centralized control. Get enhanced team management capabilities, shared Bobcoin allocation, and dedicated support to help your organization maximize productivity.\n\n**Shared Bobcoin pool**\nDistribute Bobcoins from a centralized budget to empower your entire team.\n\n**Admin dashboard**\nControl spending, manage users, and track usage across your organization.\n\n**Enterprise support**\nGet assistance from dedicated IBM Bob specialists.\n\n**Scale on demand**\nPurchase additional Bobcoin packs as your team grows.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Plan overview"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.014957086
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/shell/getting-started/start-bobshell-non-interactive_public.md_chunk_0",
          "score": 6.056065,
          "content": "Non-interactive session provide a method to use Bob Shell directly from the command line without entering an interactive session. Use for automation, scripting, and batch processing tasks.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Starting a non-interactive session"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.011288074
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "results": [
    {
      "query": "task breadcrumb history view completed task summary",
      "results": [
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/core-concepts/tools_public.md_chunk_9",
          "score": 0.7669935,
          "content": "A subtask is a fully independent task that appears in the UI with its own breadcrumb and conversation history. Unlike a subagent — which runs silently in the background and only returns a summary — a subtask is visible and interactive. You can follow its progress, review its output, and continue the conversation within it.\n\nBob uses subtasks when a request is complex enough to benefit from dedicated tracking and a separate conversation thread. A subtask can be given:\n\n- A **title** shown in the UI breadcrumb\n- A **message** with detailed instructions\n- An initial **todo list** to track steps\n- An optional **mode** to start it in (for example, Plan mode for design work)",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Tools \u003e Tool categories \u003e Subtask tools \u003e How subtasks work"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.36060163
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/configuration/carbon-modes/carbon-react-walkthrough_public.md_chunk_7",
          "score": 0.78375673,
          "content": "Provide additional details about specific components:\n\n```\nIn the breadcrumb Setup API is the current page.\nThe card components is a ClickableTile, the icon is the Connect icon.\nEstimate the grid layout and sizes for the sections and components.\n```\n\nBob will analyze the grid layout and responsive behavior:\n\n![Carbon React grid analysis](/images/carbon-react-results.png)",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Building with Carbon React mode \u003e Walkthrough: Building a Setup API page \u003e Step 3: Clarify component details"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.00014091856
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/core-concepts/tools_public.md_chunk_7",
          "score": 11.810593,
          "content": "A subagent runs independently from the main conversation. It has its own context window, executes its assigned task, and returns a summary of results back to Bob. This makes subagents useful for work that would otherwise add large amounts of irrelevant content to the main conversation.\n\nBob uses subagents sparingly. The default is always to do the work directly. A subagent is only considered when all of the following are true:\n\n- The task is clearly self-contained and only a summary is needed back\n- It would add significant irrelevant content to the main context\n- It cannot be accomplished with one or two direct tool calls\n\nThere are two subagent types:\n\nThe `explore` type provides read-only codebase exploration and runs on a lighter model.\nThe `general` type offers full tool access and runs on the default model.\nBy default, a subagent does not see the parent conversation history. Setting `fork_context: true` passes the conversation history into the subagent when it needs to understand prior decisions, constraints, or user preferences.\n\nBob does **not** use subagents for simple file reads, quick searches, single-tool operations, or tasks where it already has the relevant context.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Tools \u003e Tool categories \u003e Subagent tools \u003e How subagents work"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.00006253713
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/shell/configuration/telemetry-data-shell_public.md_chunk_1",
          "score": 9.074367,
          "content": "The table below summarizes the categories of usage data that IBM Bob can collect.\n\nRecords the action of a task being opened, completed, or reopened.\nRecords the generation, acceptance, or rejection of inline code completion.\nRecords the initiation, successful completion, or failure of a scheduled scan.\nRecords confirmation, clarification, or approval of tasks.\nRecords the action of a message being sent from you to Bob or Bob to you.\nRecords the use of tool invocations, such as `execute_command`, `read_file`, and others.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Telemetry data \u003e Collected data"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.00004870464
        },
        {
          "id": "docs/ide/core-concepts/tools_public.md_chunk_14",
          "score": 11.706545,
          "content": "Todo tools let Bob track progress through multi-step tasks using a visible checklist.\n\nThe `update_todo_list` tool creates or updates a markdown checklist of task steps to track implementation steps, mark completed items, and add newly discovered tasks.\nBob uses todo tracking for complex tasks with multiple steps. The list is updated as work progresses — marking items complete, adding newly discovered steps, and keeping one item in progress at a time. This gives you a clear view of what has been done and what remains.",
          "metadata": {
            "section_path": "Tools \u003e Tool categories \u003e Todo tools"
          },
          "rerank_score": 0.00003822911
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
